kompilator answers where it wrote each binary, and refuses a binary that was never built with the command that builds it

A caller that wanted the binary of a kompilator build had to spell the
output directory itself, `<package>/.build/kompilator/<profile>/<name>`, and
when no build had written it there, `ls` and the binary failed with "No such
file or directory", which says neither that nothing was built nor how to
build it.

`kompilator build` now checks that every binary it was asked for is in its
output and prints each one's path on standard output, one per line; its
progress stays on standard error. `kompilator where --manifest-path <dir>/
Cargo.toml [--release] [--bin NAME]... [--out DIR]` prints the same paths
without building, and refuses a binary that is not there with
"<name> has not been built: <path> does not exist; `kompilator build ...`
builds it there". A `--bin` the package does not declare is refused with the
binaries it declares, and `where` refuses a flag that changes what is
compiled rather than where it goes. The output directory is decided in one
place, binaries::out_dir, which both verbs read. The README documents the
verb, the answer and every new refusal; tests/kompilator/where.mjs is the
real journey (KOMPILATOR_BIN), not run here: building and testing waits for
the daily routine.

